# 🐳 Dockerize & Deploy Application to Docker Hub for Local Use to Another User

This project demonstrates how to **Dockerize an application, upload the Docker image to Docker Hub, and allow another user to download and run the same application on their local machine.**

## 1. Login to Docker Hub

Login to Docker Hub from the terminal:

```powershell
docker login
```

Complete the browser-based authentication.

## 2. Build the Docker Image

Build your Docker image:

```powershell
docker build -t saishshaikh23/test-image .
```

Here:

* `saishshaikh23` → Your Docker Hub username
* `test-image` → Your Docker image/repository name
* `.` → Current project directory

> Replace `saishshaikh23` with your own Docker Hub username.

## 3. Push Image to Docker Hub

Upload the Docker image to your Docker Hub repository:

```powershell
docker push saishshaikh23/test-image
```

This publishes the Docker image to Docker Hub so that other users can access and use it.

## 4. Pull Image from Docker Hub

Another user can download the image on their local machine using:

```powershell
docker pull saishshaikh23/test-image
```

This downloads the Docker image from Docker Hub.

## 5. Run the Container

After pulling the image, run the application:

```powershell
docker run saishshaikh23/test-image
```

If the application uses port `3000`, run:

```powershell
docker run -p 3000:3000 saishshaikh23/test-image
```

## 🔄 Docker Workflow

```text
Application
     ↓
Dockerfile
     ↓
Build Image
     ↓
Push to Docker Hub
     ↓
Another User
     ↓
Pull Image
     ↓
Run Container Locally
```

## 🎯 Project Goal

The goal is to package an application into a Docker image and **share it through Docker Hub so another user can easily pull and run the application on their own local machine.**


#########🐳 Docker Compose – Build, Run & Push Multiple Containers Together############
What is Docker Compose?

Docker Compose is a tool that allows us to define and manage multiple Docker containers/services using a single compose.yaml file.

Instead of building and running each container one by one, Docker Compose allows us to manage all services together.

For example, our project has:

Frontend
   +
Backend

Docker Compose can build and start both containers with a single command.

                Docker Compose
                      ↓
          ┌───────────┴───────────┐
          ↓                       ↓
      Frontend                  Backend
      Container                 Container

📁 Project Structure

Example project structure:

PHASE-2/
│
├── compose.yaml
│
├── BACKEND/
│   ├── Dockerfile
│   ├── package.json
│   └── index.js
│
└── FRONTEND/
    ├── Dockerfile
    ├── package.json
    └── src/
📝 compose.yaml

Example:

services:

  backend:
    build: ./BACKEND
    container_name: backend
    ports:
      - "8000:8000"

  frontend:
    build: ./FRONTEND
    container_name: frontend
    ports:
      - "5173:5173"

This tells Docker Compose that our project contains two services:

backend
frontend

Open the terminal inside the folder containing compose.yaml:
docker compose up    =  uss folder ko open kro jisme compose.yml he usk terminl me chalo
docker compose up --build  =   agr phirse chng kiaya compose yml file me to ye val cmnd
 
🛑 Stop All Containers
docker compose down

This stops and removes the containers created by Compose.

